INT. COURTROOM - CONVERTED DAIRY BARN - DAY

Hay bales along the walls. Sunlight slants through slatted boards. The judge's bench is a repurposed milking stand. A cow-shaped weathervane turns slowly overhead, visible through a skylight.

JUDGE WILHELMINA OKAFOR-REYES, 60s, black robe over a flannel shirt, taps a gavel made from a horseshoe. At counsel table, ATTORNEY SILAS BOUCHARD, 40s, polished brogues streaked with straw, and ATTORNEY AMARA DELACROIX-TANG, 30s, tailored suit, a new briefcase.

WILHELMINA
Counsel. The hearing is on the matter of the Henderson water rights. Mr. Bouchard.

SILAS
Your Honor, my client asserts the creek flows east.

AMARA
It flows west.

SILAS
East.

AMARA
West, Your Honor.

SILAS
Amara, I walked the creek.

AMARA
Silas, I walked it twice.

WILHELMINA
Counsel, address the bench, not each other.

SILAS
Apologies, Your Honor.

AMARA
Apologies.

Both speak at the same moment, voices stacking.

SILAS
We would move to submit the survey.

AMARA ^
We would move to submit the survey.

A long silence. A cow lows, somewhere outside.

WILHELMINA
Counsel, you appear to agree.

SILAS
On the survey, Your Honor.

AMARA
On the survey.

WILHELMINA
Then the clerk shall mark it as Exhibit A. Mr. Bouchard, please proceed.

He rises. Adjusts his tie. Stray hay falls from his sleeve.
